test.yaml:7:28: "tee --append" is rejected by the tee of macOS ("illegal option -- -"): it has only short options. The job runs on macOS (macos-latest), whose tee is the BSD one. Use "tee -a". [macos-gnu-flags]
test.yaml:8:18: "sed -i" without a suffix takes the script as the suffix of the backup on macOS, so the file is read as the script ("invalid command code"). The job runs on macOS (macos-latest), whose sed is the BSD one. Use "sed -i.bak" (and remove the backup), which both seds understand, or "sed -i ''" on macOS only. [macos-gnu-flags]
test.yaml:9:18: "sed -i" without a suffix takes the script as the suffix of the backup on macOS, so the file is read as the script ("invalid command code"). The job runs on macOS (macos-latest), whose sed is the BSD one. Use "sed -i.bak" (and remove the backup), which both seds understand, or "sed -i ''" on macOS only. [macos-gnu-flags]
test.yaml:10:18: "sed --in-place" is rejected by the sed of macOS: it has no long options. The job runs on macOS (macos-latest), whose sed is the BSD one. Use "sed -i.bak" (and remove the backup), which both seds understand. [macos-gnu-flags]
test.yaml:11:19: "date -d" does not exist in the date of macOS ("illegal option -- d"). The job runs on macOS (macos-latest), whose date is the BSD one. Use "date -j -f FORMAT ..." or "date -v", or install coreutils and call "gdate". [macos-gnu-flags]
test.yaml:12:19: "grep -P" is rejected by the grep of macOS ("invalid option -- P"): it has no Perl regular expressions. The job runs on macOS (macos-latest), whose grep is the BSD one. Use "grep -E" if the pattern allows it, or "perl -ne", which macOS has too. [macos-gnu-flags]
test.yaml:13:34: "grep -P" is rejected by the grep of macOS ("invalid option -- P"): it has no Perl regular expressions. The job runs on macOS (macos-latest), whose grep is the BSD one. Use "grep -E" if the pattern allows it, or "perl -ne", which macOS has too. [macos-gnu-flags]
test.yaml:14:19: "stat -c" does not exist in the stat of macOS: its format option is -f. The job runs on macOS (macos-latest), whose stat is the BSD one. Use "stat -f FORMAT" on macOS, or read the value in another way (wc -c < file for the size). [macos-gnu-flags]
test.yaml:15:34: "find -printf" is not a primary of the find of macOS ("unknown primary or operator"). The job runs on macOS (macos-latest), whose find is the BSD one. Use "-print" with other commands, or "-exec stat -f", or install findutils and call "gfind". [macos-gnu-flags]
test.yaml:16:17: "cp --parents" is rejected by the cp of macOS: it has no long options. The job runs on macOS (macos-latest), whose cp is the BSD one. Use "mkdir -p" for the directory and "cp" for the file. [macos-gnu-flags]
test.yaml:19:15: "sed -i" without a suffix takes the script as the suffix of the backup on macOS, so the file is read as the script ("invalid command code"). The job runs on macOS (macos-latest), whose sed is the BSD one. Use "sed -i.bak" (and remove the backup), which both seds understand, or "sed -i ''" on macOS only. [macos-gnu-flags]
test.yaml:20:32: "tee --append" is rejected by the tee of macOS ("illegal option -- -"): it has only short options. The job runs on macOS (macos-latest), whose tee is the BSD one. Use "tee -a". [macos-gnu-flags]
test.yaml:27:19: "date -d" does not exist in the date of macOS ("illegal option -- d"). The job runs on macOS (macos-15), whose date is the BSD one. Use "date -j -f FORMAT ..." or "date -v", or install coreutils and call "gdate". [macos-gnu-flags]
